Yes, lucky us, a TV-card with a Brooktree 787 chip will work.
I have a Hauppauge WinTV FM working under Linux in my PWS 433, which has 
that BT787. Different brands or models are likely to work as well.

The card I have also has a radio tuner, which I haven't gotten to work, 
but I really didn't try yet. I can tell you that the particular radio 
device that's on the card isn't recognized by the linux kernel. At least 
  in my case... I live in the Netherlands, so versions of the card in 
different countries may use different radio chips on a Hauppauge card (?).

Also, I'm not sure the mixer device on the card is fully functional. 
It's a MSP34xx in my case, and it is properly recognized by the kernel.
I don't know what the thing is supposed to be able to do, but for sound 
I just plugged the line-out of the TV-card into line-in of the soundcard 
(the built-in es1371).

Sound is in stereo if broadcasted signal is stereo. Watch out for 
cheaper TV-cards, as the might be limited to mono sound! Cheaper cards 
usually don't give TeleText and/or radio tuner as well. Read specs...

Furthermore, I tried the card in a 64-bits PCI slot as well as a 32-bits 
  PCI slot. It only worked in the 32-bits slot. (Yes, I have my 
PCI_OVERRIDE or whatever to -1 in my SRM)
So, you might wanna make sure it's a 32-bit slot that you still have 
left :-)

Software I used:
Debian GNU/Linux 3.0, a.k.a. Woody (hoorray for this distro!)
Kernels 2.2.21-pre2, 2.4.19-pre3 and several others before that... most 
are just fine (enough).
XFree 4.1.0 (from the distro a la dselect)
xawtv
alevt (this will give you TeleText of CeeFAX or whatever you call it)

I've also tried a few software descramblers to try and get Canal+.
These wont' work, because their sources won't compile on the 64bit 
Alpha... too bad.


I don't know about the large drives on onboard IDE, I use an old NCR 
53C875 SCSI controller with 2 SCSI disks. My SCSI-cdrom is on the same 
controller. So, I don't use the IDE at all. I don't know much detail, 
but if you have an elder generation PWS like I do, it has a supposedly 
buggy IDE chipset (CMD64x ?). Do your Google for details :-)

Dannis.


