Jeremy:

SCSI devices have unique device address, unlike IDE which you are probably more
familiar with.
Somewhere on each SCSI device (usually on the back between the SCSI cable and
power plug, but not always) are a series of jumper pins. There's usually 4 to 5
sets of pins of which 3 sets of the pins which are used to set the address.
Typically they are identified on each drive, but again, not always.  This is
what is used to set the Address.
If no pins are jumpered the address is 0.
If the first set of pins (always starting from the left) is jumpered the address
is 1.
If the second set of pins are jumpered, the address is 2.
If the third set of pins are jumpered, the address is 4.
If a jumper is on the first set and the second set the address is 3.
If a jumper is on the first set and the second set and the third set the address
is 7
(7 is typically reserved as the address of the SCSI card, so NEVER use it)
If a jumper is on the first set and the third set the address is: 5
If a jumper is on the second set and the third set the address is 6.

Each devices MUST have a unique address on a SCSI chain.
The First HD on the SCSI chain is usually 0
The Second HD is usually 1
The Third HD is usually 2
The Fourth HD is usually 3
The CDRom is usually 4
A Tape drive is usually 5

And finally, some flavors of SCSI require terminiation.  Open the computer and
examine the SCSI ribbon cable.  It will go from the SCSI card (or connector on
MB) to each drive in turn, and then stop. At the end it should have a small
plastic box plugged into the last plug, that's the terminator.  If it's not
there, that COULD be a problem, depending on what flavor of SCSI that you have.
Check the back of the computer, if it has an external SCSI connector, it MAY
need to be terminated also. There are special "connectors" which plug into these
sockets to terminate the SCSI chain.

Good luck!

TomC



