AFAIK RedHat vanilla kernels do not have support for DAC960 (at least 
not in kernel, not sure whether there is module).

We had a similar problem with a brand-new IBM server and RH6.2. Tried to 
make custom installation floppies with a kernel with built-in DAC960 
support -- didn't work.

Changed over to Debian Potato, the installation program of which allows 
foreign driver modules to be read from floppies prior to base-system 
installation -- worked perfectly.

This was actually one thing that made me to change over from RH to 
Debian. The other thing was that there just is no way to squeeze a RH 
running a 2.4-series kernel into a antiquated 386 (16 MiB RAM, 200 MiB 
HD, sbpcd CD-ROM). Changed over to Debian Woody -- worked fine. Net 
installation rox...

