Usually the compiler keeps track of alignment and will place short,integers 
and longs at the right addresses (div 2, div 4 or div 8). At least the 
Compaq compiler is doing this. If the programmer is, however, reading or 
writing to a memory segment and is not keeping track of the right alignment 
a trap will occur (indicating a penalty). This problem is as old as the 
existance of 16bit cpu's.

BTW the code produced by the Compaq compiler is at least twice as fast as 
the gcc code. That's what I found with my programms.

To come back to the original request for help with Suse 7.1: what do you 
need or what do you expect?

Cheers,
Manfred




